delay 60
text Midnight passed and I still couldn't fall asleep.
text After my father went to bed, I snuck out of the house.
text I felt I had to apologize for not going to her party.
text I felt I had to apologize for not giving her a present.
text But in truth, I didn't care about any of that.
text I just wanted to see her.
text I wanted to see the girl who would always, without fail, say "see you tomorrow."
delay 120
text Her house was cloaked in darkness, without a single light to be seen. It looked like a haunted castle.
text I gathered all of my courage and pushed through the gap in the hedge.
text The garden was frighteningly dark at this hour.
text It looked as if everything were dead.
text The table, the chairs, the flower bed... it looked like everything had been swapped with an artificial version of itself.
text The insects sounded like machines, and they seemed to be growing louder and louder.
text @[Tomoya]
text Kotomi-chan.
text I whispered her name in the darkness.
text @[Tomoya]
text Kotomi-chan, Kotomi-chan... Kotomi-chan.
text No matter how long I waited, it didn't seem like she would appear.
text The glass door was open just a crack.
text Feeling drawn to it, I slid it open and went into the living room.
text It was even darker in there than in the garden.
text The air was stagnant and cold, as if the house had been abandoned for a long time.
text I walked quietly and moved deeper.
text The guilt of doing something bad and an indescribable worry were blending inside of me.
text Had decades passed in the short time I hadn't been here?
text Was this all a dream?
text Will I never see her again?
text And then I smelled something burning.
text I heard the sounds of sobbing.
text She was crying!
text I ran upstairs.
text Almost tripping over the steep stairs, I ran upward.
text I crashed through the door at the end of the hallway.
text There she was.
text Sitting on the floor, crying.
text What appeared to be a book was burning in front of her.
text The orange flames danced as if they were alive.
text I tried to put out the fire.
text I ran to the kitchen, filled a cup with water, and went up and down the stairs repeatedly.
text Despite my efforts, the flames would not subside. They soon spread to the carpet.
text I clenched my teeth and doused the fire with water several times.
text Just as I began to think all was lost, a crowd of unfamiliar adults came into the room.
text They used their clothes to extinguish the fire.
text She was crying the whole time.
text She covered her face with her tiny palms and sat there crying.
text It was like she had forgotten how to do anything but cry.
text In an attempt to protect her, I stood between her and the adults.
text I expected them to yell at her for playing with fire.
text The adults tried to gather the charred pieces of paper.
text Then they bent down and softly spoke to her.
sound VYS.aac
text "This wasn't it."
sound VYT.aac
text "You don't need to cry."
sound VYV.aac
text "This was something for you."
text But she didn't stop crying.
text They all looked really sad.
text There was no way for me to know what had happened.
text But I knew one thing...
text The smell of the tobacco pipe, the bright white apron...
text The beautiful garden, the mysterious girl...
text All of that was gone.
